# Spec-Driven Development Guidelines

This repository uses Spec-Driven Development (SDD) for medium-to-large changes. The goal is to make work executable by agents without losing product intent, resource contracts, data/secret dependencies, or verification requirements.

This is a Helm chart repository that deploys Kubernetes custom resources (`asgard-ai.com/v1alpha1`) through the platform's pipeline. "Implementation" here means changes to CRs, Helm templates, agents/skills and `.asgard-pipeline.yaml` — not application source code.

Two things about this repo shape the specs you write:

- **The databases are real.** Every source this repo reads is one of the customer's live business systems, not a seeded demo. A spec that touches a `SemanticLayer` must state which cubes/tables it relies on and confirm the query is read-only. Use the `semantic-layer-modeling` skill to introspect the actual schema instead of guessing.
- **Every source system is read-only until a spec says otherwise.** This is the standing architecture: reads go through a `SemanticLayer` or a fixed query tool, and any write path points *outward* (a notification endpoint), never at a source system. Adding a write path to a source system is a behaviour change and needs a spec. Record the invariant list in `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/architecture.md` as the system takes shape.

## Document Layers

Four layers, and **tense is the only reason they are separate directories**:

```
docs/meeting-notes/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md   raw input (discussion before it converges)
        │ converge
        ▼
docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md       dated decision record (immutable — "why we chose this")
        │ apply delta
        ▼
docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/<module>.md     ★ living spec ("what the system does NOW")
        │ break into work
        ▼
requirements/tasks/TASK-xxx-*.md           executable task spec (EARS criteria + tasks + log)
        │ implement
        ▼
projects/<project>/chart/                  the Asgard CRs
```

| Layer | Tense | Answers |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/meeting-notes/` | before convergence | "what was actually discussed" |
| `docs/decisions/` | a snapshot, **immutable** | "why was it decided this way" |
| `docs/spec/` | **always current** | "what is the agreed behaviour today" |
| `requirements/tasks/` | a work item's lifecycle | "how is this change done and verified" |

Two rules keep the layers from collapsing into each other:

- **The living spec carries no history.** "It used to be A, now it's B" belongs in a decision
  record; the living spec only says B.
- **A decision record is never edited** once written. Changed your mind → write a new one.

`docs/README.md` is the index for all of this, and it also draws the line against `AGENTS.md`:
`AGENTS.md` answers *how to change this repo*, `docs/spec/` answers *what the system does*.
Write each fact on one side only.

## Directory Roles

- `docs/spec/<slug>/`: **living spec** — the current, single source of truth for system behaviour.
  Read it before writing a task spec; update it when a decision changes behaviour.
- `docs/decisions/`: dated, immutable decision records. Read them when you need to know why.
- `docs/meeting-notes/`: raw discussion, before convergence. Optional — a GitHub issue or a direct
  user instruction can be cited straight from the decision record.
- `references/`: background material only. Use it for external API documentation, vendor docs, and
  exploratory research.
- `requirements/`: implementation source of truth. Agents must implement from requirements, not
  directly from references.
- `requirements/requests/`: high-level requirements, governance documents, cross-task background, and links to related task specs.
- `requirements/tasks/`: executable task specs. Each task must be specific enough for an agent to implement and verify.

If `references/` conflicts with `requirements/`, trust `requirements/`. Record the conflict as an open question or decision before implementation.

If the **living spec** conflicts with a task spec, the living spec describes what is deployed and
the task spec describes what is being changed — reconcile them by finishing the task and applying
its delta to the living spec, not by silently editing one to match the other.

## When SDD Is Required

Use SDD before changing a chart when the work involves any of the following:

- New agent, skill/skillset wiring, or multi-step behavior change.
- New custom resource (`Agent`, `Toolset`, `Workflow`, `SkillSet`, `SourceSet`, `Syncer`, `SandboxBlueprint`, `BotProvider`, `SemanticLayer`, `DataConnector`, `CompletionModel`).
- **Any new `SemanticLayer` / `DataConnector`, or a change to which cubes an agent may query** — these point at real databases.
- **Re-introducing a write path** (`Toolset` → `Workflow` → `query-database`), which also means deciding the `requestConsent` gate.
- New or changed external API contract, request/response shape, error handling, or auth/secret usage.
- Model selection or prompt change that affects agent behavior.
- A release, its trigger, its chart or its declared keys changing in `.asgard-pipeline.yaml`.
- Adding a new project under `projects/`.
- Ambiguous acceptance criteria or unresolved product decisions.

Small fixes, one-file config changes, or exact user-instructed changes may skip full SDD, but must still obey existing requirements if present.

## Status Flow

Use exactly these status values: `draft` → `ready` → `in-progress` → `done`.

Do not use `in_progress`. Do not move `ready` to `in-progress` until the user explicitly instructs implementation to begin.

## Required Indexes

- `requirements/_index.md`: repository-level requirements entrypoint and SDD convention summary.
- `requirements/requests/_index.md`: high-level requirements registry.
- `requirements/tasks/_index.md`: executable task registry.

When adding or changing a request/task status, update the matching index in the same change.

## Task Spec Format

Use single-file task specs under `requirements/tasks/TASK-xxx-short-name.md`, each with:

- `Meta`: task ID, status, priority, spec mode, related request/reference links.
- `1) Requirements`: background, goal, in scope, out of scope, known context, open questions/decisions, EARS acceptance criteria.
- `2) Design`: resource/template structure, data & secret dependencies, external API contracts, auth notes, acceptance test matrix, verification plan.
- `3) Implementation Tasks`: reviewable tasks mapped to acceptance criteria.
- `4) Execution Log / Change Log`: spec creation, decisions, status changes, implementation notes, verification results.

## Acceptance Criteria

Use EARS-style acceptance criteria with `R#` identifiers. Each `R#` must map to at least one implementation task and at least one acceptance test or verification case.

## Readiness Gate

A task can move from `draft` to `ready` only when background/goal/scope are clear, blocking questions are resolved, resource behavior and error states are defined, external API/auth/secret requirements are defined when applicable, and every `R#` maps to implementation tasks and a verification entry.

## Done Gate

A task can move to `done` only when all acceptance criteria are satisfied, the repo's four verification gates are green (see below), no unapproved spec deviation remains, and task spec + index statuses are synchronized.

**Plus the two document-layer obligations**, which is what keeps the living spec from going stale:

- If the task changed **behaviour**, apply the delta to `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/<module>.md`.
- If the task settled a **decision** (a rejected alternative, a trade-off, a corrected assumption),
  write `docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` for it. One decision per file.

A task whose behaviour change never reached the living spec is not done — the next reader will
implement against a spec that no longer describes the system.

## Verification Gates

This repo's gate is **one command**, `asgard-cli gate`; run it after changing anything. What the PLATFORM checks is the other half and the authoritative one - the `asgard-cr-verification` skill under `.agents/skills/` documents it, and `asgard-cli skill update` is what writes that skill, from the platform, so it describes the server this repository deploys to:

```bash
# 1. structural repo consistency (README registry, .asgard-pipeline.yaml, assets/skills,
#    requirements indexes, and the docs/ spec structure)
asgard-cli check [project]

# 2. chart static validation
asgard-cli gate                                 # lint + render + xref + the reference material
#    the lint step is inside gate, which supplies the reserved asgard values file.
#    A bare `helm lint <chart>` supplies nothing and fails on every chart that labels anything.

# 3. render + CR cross-reference completeness, then the agent-split invariants
asgard-cli verify <project>

# 4. against the cluster you deploy to (read-only, needs kubectl)
asgard-cli render <release> | kubectl apply --dry-run=server -n <ns> -f -
asgard-cli pipeline runs watch --release <name> --ref <tag>
```

Step 3 catches what `helm lint` cannot: an `Agent` referencing a `SemanticLayer`/`Toolset` that does not exist, a `Toolset` pointing at a missing `Workflow`, a `SemanticLayer` pointing at a missing `DataConnector`, a wrong `entry` name on any entrypoint, and the Platform UI metadata whose absence is invisible to every other check.

Step 4 runs on the platform and cannot be run here: no cluster credential is issued to a client. Its dry run answers both questions that used to need two commands — `crd/dry-run-rejected` is "will it be accepted", `crd/unknown-field` is "will it be kept". A CRD silently prunes undeclared fields and a plain dry-run still reports success — `.agents/skills/asgard-platform/wiki/crd-rules.md` has the release it broke.

Step 1 also checks the `docs/` layer: required files, the living spec module index matching the actual module files, dated record filenames, and every relative markdown link inside `docs/` resolving.

Note `asgard-cli render` **only renders**. Deployment is CD-only (push a tag) — see `AGENTS.md` → Deploy / CI for why a local `helm upgrade` produces a broken Syncer.

## Bootstrap Guidance

Keep these SDD files unless the user explicitly opts out:

```text
docs/README.md
docs/spec/README.md
docs/spec/<slug>/README.md
docs/decisions/README.md
docs/decisions/_decision-template.md
docs/meeting-notes/README.md
docs/meeting-notes/_template.md
references/README.md
requirements/README.md
requirements/_index.md
requirements/requests/_index.md
requirements/tasks/_index.md
```
